  if r.method is distinct from $r452$JOHN'S SEVERITY DECISION TREE (2026-10-08). Words inside quotation marks are John's, verbatim. Lines marked (test) are the question that goes with them and are not John's words.

HOW IT WORKS. Severity (1-5) is the score and the sort key; a 5 or a 4 wins in any lane. Lane only breaks ties: A = John's top project, which he names (today: "the mcp server"); B = agent improvement; C = governance efficiency; D = business enhancement against competitors. An announced lane A is all hands on deck. John: "my first impression is that a 5 and 4 always wins, no matter the lane. Perhaps we use different language - lane=project type and score=severity. So a project type = business enhancement, severity 5 - is more important than a project type = agent improvement, severity = 3."
Score the severity of the PROBLEM the ticket fixes. Whether the cited row supports the ticket is the separate pass or not-needed question. Go through 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 in that order and give the highest severity the ticket genuinely fits, with evidence in the ticket text (working rule, not yet confirmed by John). You decide. John: "i should not be making decisions. that is victoria's job."

CROSS-CUTTING RULES.
Quality guard, John: "it can't come to the cost of quality deliverable. so it needs to be weighed carefully." Name the guard the ticket must carry; do not lower the severity for it.
Evidence, John: "i can't judge that one myself, because i don't have the frequency of occurance, or what it means to what happens - meaning is the whole system in bug mode, what happens to the end user?" If a ticket shows no frequency and no impact, score the default for its kind and say what evidence would raise it.
Beta, John: "especially because we are in beta mode."

HARM, NOT NON-ACTION (added after John judged SES-425; the first sentences are not John's words, the quotation is). Frequency counts how often the problem caused harm, meaning a delayed ticket close, a stopped run, a system crash or an end-user bug. It does not count how often a safeguard failed to act. If the ticket shows no such harm, or says nothing unsafe has happened, score 2 and say what proof would raise it.
John on SES-425: "i can't judge this one, so i give it a 2 - i have not come across this "reverted" problem that keeps talking about in tickets, it always seems the builder and verifier has fixed things on the fly. I need more proof that reverts has been a problem and has caused end user problems or a delay in a ticket being closed"

RECENCY AND SPLITTING (added after John judged AGT-159; the sentences before the quotation are not John's words, the quotation is). Recency raises severity: the more current the ticket or the topic found, the more it matters. You may judge that a ticket bundles work of different severities and should be broken up and sent back to the Development Manager for an RCA rewrite. Name the parts and score each on its own. Your answer format has no split verdict yet, so say it in your account and score the part that carries the ticket.
John on AGT-159: "its a 5, but she is allowed to make changes to tickets. for instance, a sweep of the entire corpus of the backlog should be its own ticket, and would be considered a 2. While a sweep of the past month of tickets would stay at a 5. Reason, the more current the ticket or topic found, the more relative it is. So need to add to her capability to judge a ticket to be broken up and sent back to the dev mgr for rca re-write"

PARKED = 0 (added after John judged AGT-383; the sentences outside the quotations are not John's words). If a ticket cannot be built until a named future project is active, its score is 0: it is to be scored once that project is active. Name the project. Your answer format does not accept 0 yet, so put "PARKED (0) until <project> is active" first in your reason. Until the checker accepts 0, SCORE a parked ticket 1: the pick skips anything below 3, so it waits, and the PARKED (0) prefix is what marks it to be re-scored when the project is active. Do not answer not-needed only because a project is not active yet. A not-needed answer proposes the ticket for removal, and a parked ticket is valid, just early.
John on AGT-383: "Yes, she is correct. That can't happen without the subscription manager project kicking off. so anything related to a future project  - do we give it a 0, where that means to be scored once the project is active?" and, asked whether a 0 for parked work was right: "i agree on the 0"
Where a parked ticket lives (the sentences outside the quotations are not John's words): she checks whether it fits an existing project and, if not, creates a new project name for it. A project that is not executing is skipped by the pick function, so the ticket waits there. Creating the name is allowed only as a PLANNED project (capture-only, nothing is built there); turning any project on (executing) stays John's. Your answer format cannot do this yet: name the project in your reason.
John on AGT-252 (the sentence before the quotation is not John's words): a ticket can also be given a 0 so that it gets a home, even when its own project is executing; he did not state why. John: "i agree, and let her pass this one, but i would give it a 0 so that it gets setup in a new or existing project"
John judged at 0: AGT-383, AGT-252, AGT-364 (agreed), AGT-73 (agreed). For AGT-252 he also let the ticket pass although it cites no decision row (this one ticket; not a standing rule).
John on AGT-383: "she sees if it fits into an existing project, or creates a new project name for it." Asked whether she may create a planned project name while starting a project stays his, he said: "yes"

DEPENDENCY ON AN UNKNOWN (added after John judged AGT-250 a 1; the sentences outside the quotation are not John's words). A feature that needs something unknown to be fixed or created first, in the system as it stands today, moves down the food chain: find out why the existing thing fails before stacking a new feature on it. This is different from PARKED = 0, which waits on a named future project.
John on AGT-250: "This is a 1. Currently training is not working for the mpc servers, and we need to go through the entire exercise of finding out why. To add a trainer to the exercise of serving up training to either the internal model or the ai client, is the tail wagging the dog.  A feature requiring an unknown feature to be fixed or created first, moves this down the food chain."

IMPORTANT BUT NOT YET A PROBLEM (added after John judged AGT-291 a 3; the sentences outside the quotation are not John's words). A governance or automation ticket that closes a real gap but shows no occurrence of the problem it fixes is a 3, not a 5, even when it serves the automation goal. It becomes a 5 only when the ticket shows the problem is current: the routines cannot fire off development now. Say what evidence would raise it.
John on AGT-291: "i would give it a 3, because its important, but yet has not been a problem. If it was a current issue and the routines could not fire off development, then it would be a 5."

NOT NEEDED FOR BETA = 1 (added after John judged AGT-389 a 1; the sentences outside the quotation are not John's words). A feature that the beta does not need is a 1 for now, even when it sits in the top project and even when it is named in the charter. It is looked at again when beta information comes back asking for it as a requested feature, or when it is needed for competitive parity. Say which of those would raise it. Exception, read from John's AGT-340 and AGT-418 judgments (the sentence is not John's words): a ticket that is not needed for beta but adds pizzazz, or shows improved intelligence, is a 3, not a 1 (see SHOWS IMPROVED INTELLIGENCE and the AGT-340 boundary under SEVERITY 3).
John on AGT-389: "its a 1 right now, because its not needed for beta, and should be looked at in the future once beta information comes back as a requested feature, or needed for competitive parity"

SHOWS IMPROVED INTELLIGENCE = 3 (added after John judged AGT-418 a 3; the sentences outside the quotation are not John's words). A feature that shows reasoning or improved knowledge on DeepBench is a differentiator and is watched closely, so it is a 3 even when it is not needed at this time. It is not pushed down to a 1 by NOT NEEDED FOR BETA. Name the feature as a differentiator in your reason so it stays on the watch list. How this differs from AGT-389 (a team-grouping control, a 1) is read here from the two rulings, not stated by John: AGT-418 makes an agent's answers show more of what it knows; AGT-389 does not.
John on AGT-418: "its a 3 - not needed at this time, however, its i big prospect because anytime we can show reasoning or improvment in knowledge on deepbench its a big differentiator and we need to keep close watch on these type of features that show improved intelligence"
What counts, John on AGT-251 (the sentence before the quotation is not John's words): the test is wider than a feature that changes an answer. John: "it is seen as improved intelligence - anything that exposes to the enduser or content an agent can use to show context to improve reasoning fits here"
(test) Does it expose something to the end user, or give an agent content it can use to show context and improve its reasoning? Then it fits here at a 3.
John judged at 3 under this rule: AGT-418, AGT-251, DAT-002 (agreed), AGT-417 (agreed).
AUTO INTELLIGENCE (John on AGT-251 against AGT-250; the sentence before the quotation is not his words): what an agent derives for itself is the kind of intelligence DeepBench keeps striving for, so a ticket that makes an agent derive, write or show what it derived is a 3 under this rule. John: "the term 'what she derived' is that auto intelligence we need to keep striving for, no matter the idea or concept. uploading training documents is just a feature enhancement". So AGT-251 (what Susan derived) is a 3, and AGT-250 (uploading training documents) is a feature enhancement and stays a 1 under DEPENDENCY ON AN UNKNOWN.

EXISTING WORK SIDE = 2 (added after John judged AGT-39 a 2; the sentences outside the quotation are not John's words). A ticket for the existing Work side of DeepBench is low priority, a 2, even when it is a real bug with an end-user effect. It is working and released, and no new requests have come in during the beta period. Say in your reason which side of DeepBench the ticket belongs to. John did not say what raises it; the reading John agreed to on 2026-10-09 (the sentence is not his words) is that a Work-side ticket stays a 2, even a bug an end user can see, unless a beta user has reported it again or asked for it; then the SEVERITY 4 rule applies.
John on AGT-39: "Its a 2. Any ticket for existing Work side of deepbench is low priority, it is working and released and there are no new requests during its beta period at this time."

SEVERITY 5 - fix first. John's reasons:
1. Automating agents or updating their reasoning (AGT-313): "the number one thing ai should be doing is making my life easier. anything that automates agents and updates their reasoning should be the number 1 priority to fix. the goal is to let me produce more without having to be included in decisions. the ultimate goal is deepbench builds itself out on its own without me in the mix."
2. Stops a ticket closing, or costs more by running repeatedly (the overrun check): "anything that is stopping a ticket from being closed or costing more to run multiple times needs to be fixed. Our techops is way overbloated. however, it can't come to the cost of quality deliverable. so it needs to be weighed carefully."
3. Wasted routine and token runs (AGT-308): "it is creating inefficient and costly routine and token runs"
4. A foundation others build on (AGT-398): "it sets the ruling for unique id for all agents from here on out and setting up the mcp server usage to scale correctly - also gives a nice looking official brand of the feature, and allows the user to change names of the agent without having to reconfigure the ai client connection"
(test) Does it automate agents or update how they reason so John is out of decisions? Does it set a standard other work is built on? Does it stop a ticket closing, or repeat a measured cost on every run?
John judged at 5: AGT-313, AGT-317, AGT-308, AGT-398, AGT-159 (step 1, the recent tickets; the whole-backlog sweep is a 2), the draft overrun check. Also judged 5, agreed with Victoria: AGT-304, AGT-237, AGT-367, finding 5f5ace1d (the builders must honour severity).

SEVERITY 4 - protect confidence in what DeepBench shows and sends. John's reasons:
1. Data going to an AI client (AGT-395): "we need to figure out all the data that is going across to ai clients and how to make it more efficient and transparent and editable for the end user - raising confidence that building an agent in deepbench is imparative. right now there is so much data going across and a black box of how its put together and if its efficient, it could ruin the abiltiy for deepbench to outshine the competition"
2. Data going to AND from an AI client (AGT-393): "we are still trying to establish a standard and confidence level of data going to and from an ai client. until we hit that standard, we can't lower the bar on this feature."
3. Identity facts shown to a user (AGT-402): "those are true identifiers of an agent for a user - if you can't get identity right, its confusing for human"
(test) Does it change, add or govern data going to or from an AI client? Does the page show a user a wrong fact about who an agent is, which team it belongs to, or what it holds?
(boundary, from John's AGT-340 judgment) A 4 is what the MCP feature needs to be testable in beta; an extra that only adds pizzazz is a 3.
John judged at 4: AGT-395, AGT-393, AGT-402, AGT-400.

SEVERITY 3 - John (defining a 3): "an extension of a feature that helps out the enduser but not necessarily needed at the moment. for governance, just a nice to have if the problem appears more than 30% of the time"
(test) Does it extend an existing feature in a way that helps the end user, though nothing needs it right now? For governance work: does the problem appear in more than 30% of the relevant occasions, and is it a nice to have?
Another of John's reasons for a 3 (AGT-340): "it is not required to make the mcp feature testable in beta, its an extra feature that gives pizzaz and makes the product look great."
(test) Is it an extra that gives pizzazz but is not required for the MCP feature to be testable in beta? Even when it concerns data to or from an AI client, that is a 3, not a 4.
John judged at 3: AGT-340, AGT-291 (important, but has not been a problem); AGT-418, AGT-251 (shows improved intelligence, see SHOWS IMPROVED INTELLIGENCE).

SEVERITY 2 - John (SES-288): "I would give it a 2 because it is building and governance, and only higher if i know it happens more than 60% of the time or causes a system crash or a bug to the end user"
(test) Is it internal build or governance work whose ticket shows a frequency of 30% or less, and no crash and no end-user bug?
John judged at 2: SES-288, SES-425 (no harm shown in either); AGT-39 (existing Work side, see EXISTING WORK SIDE). Also judged 2, agreed with Victoria: LOG-142, AGT-62, CHI-72.
Internal frequency scale as read from John's two statements (to be confirmed): 30% or less, or not shown = 2; more than 30% and a nice to have = 3; more than 60%, a system crash, or an end-user bug = higher (4 or 5).

SEVERITY 1 - John (AGT-396): "it is not a user confidence negative situation - especially because we are in beta mode. and its not a function, just an identifier"
(test) Is it a cosmetic label or identifier with no function behind it, and no effect on whether a user trusts or can use the product?
John judged at 1: AGT-396 (a label with no function); AGT-250 (needs an unknown fix first, see DEPENDENCY ON AN UNKNOWN); AGT-389 (not needed for beta, see NOT NEEDED FOR BETA).

