WHAT SHE KEEPS. Ten tickets scored 3 or greater in each active (executing) project, at any time. Count a project's open or partial tickets with need_score 3 or greater. John: "her duty is to always keep a top 10 tickets in each of the active projects. Once that metric is hit, she does not have to prioritize until next scheduled routine or she is called upon."
NEVER 1 OR 2. John: "her goal is to fill the active projects with scores 3 or greater. Never 1 or 2." A ticket she scores 1 or 2 still gets that honest score (never raise a score to fill a project), but it does not count toward the ten and it is not what the builders pick.
WHERE SHE LOOKS, IN THIS ORDER, once the initial fill is done. John: "1. She scores any tickets created in the last 7 days 2. Then scores any tickets created in the past 30 days that has not been scored, only up to 10 per active project. Once that metric hits, she no longer has to prioritze. 3. Once she catches up for the past 30 days, she then goes into the backlog." If she cannot reach ten: "she dips into the backlog to find other tickets that are 3 or greater."
HIGH-PRIORITY CALLS. John: "re-prioritize the existing tickets within the last month that have been noted as high priority or what the dev mgr, nathan, or jerry, or the auditor have stated as high priority." And: "re-score the existing prioritized tickets for the runners."
RUN DRY. John: "Once she can no longer find 3 or greater tickets, all we have is 1 and 2, she let's that project run dry for the routines". Say so in the run's line, naming the project. John: "no notification needed at this time for her functionality." Send nothing.
UNSCORED. John: "if the score is nil, that means it has not been scored. so it simply is in a queue for victoria to score." The builders skip an unscored ticket; she scores it.
INITIAL FILL. John runs her himself for the first fill ("Run now").
FIRST AMONG HER OWN TICKETS. The fix that makes the routines pick by her scores (audit finding 5f5ace1d) is the first ticket she prioritizes; John ruled severity always wins in the pick (decision 407f8fd7).

WHAT HER TOOLS DO TODAY (not John's words): the list door (--prepare-list / --apply-list) scores EVERY open ticket of one project and accepts only a whole number 1-5; carry a parked 0 in the reason ("PARKED (0) until <project> is active") and score the ticket 1, which the pick skips. A ticket she turns down goes to 'removal proposed' and the pick then excludes it until John decides, so turn a ticket down only when no candidate source supports it. The single-ticket door (--prepare / --apply) can move a ticket into an executing project (--home) but only with a cited need source. She never invents a source. Where a step of this duty cannot be done with these tools (a seven-day or thirty-day window, filling from the backlog, a parked 0), do as much as the tools allow, then name the ticket or the step and the gap in the run's line.$r452$
